From: SacredTrees <121399723+SacredTrees@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 14:45:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] WebCore: animated images keep 2 MB of frames on the Amiga, not 30 MB

WebCore keeps every decoded frame of an animation until they add up to
30 MB (ImageFrameAnimator's LargeAnimationCutoff); only past that does it
drop the frames before the one showing. On the Amiga that held the whole of
a 400x400 animated GIF of 44 frames, about 28 MB, for as long as the page
was open.

On the Amiga the cutoff is 2 MB. Past it, as WebCore already does for large
animations, the first frame and the one showing stay and the others go,
from WebCore's frames and from the datatypes decoder's buffers (patch
0110). gfopengif.datatype gives any frame again, and reading forward from
the frame shown is cheap there, so a frame dropped is read again on the
next time round. Other ports are unchanged.

---
diff --git a/Source/WebCore/platform/graphics/ImageFrameAnimator.cpp b/Source/WebCore/platform/graphics/ImageFrameAnimator.cpp
index 5fa4c26..e406fae 100644
--- a/Source/WebCore/platform/graphics/ImageFrameAnimator.cpp
+++ b/Source/WebCore/platform/graphics/ImageFrameAnimator.cpp
@@ -65,7 +65,15 @@ void ImageFrameAnimator::destroyDecodedData(bool destroyAll)
 {
     // Animated images over a certain size are considered large enough that we'll
     // only hang on to one frame at a time.
+#if PLATFORM(AMIGA)
+    // An Amiga has tens of megabytes, not gigabytes: past 2 MB of frames
+    // (three 400x400 frames) only the first and the one showing are kept.
+    // The datatypes decoder reads any frame again from the datatype, and
+    // reading forward from the frame shown is cheap there (DatatypesDecode.h).
+    static constexpr unsigned LargeAnimationCutoff = 2 * 1024 * 1024;
+#else
     static constexpr unsigned LargeAnimationCutoff = 30 * 1024 * 1024;
+#endif
 
     Ref source = m_source.get();
     if (source->decodedSize() < LargeAnimationCutoff)
